WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.495
Every time you rise from a squat, your knees carry most of the load.

00:00:04.495 --> 00:00:11.016
Exoskeletons are wearable robots that can share it, adding torque at the joint right when your muscles need it.

00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:15.661
The most advanced ones don't replay a fixed pattern.

00:00:15.661 --> 00:00:22.189
They estimate your own effort, moment by moment, from sensors on the device, and assist in proportion.

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:27.693
This approach, called biological torque control, has already made walking less tiring.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.246
But there's a catch.

00:00:30.246 --> 00:00:40.402
To learn your effort, the controller is trained on large amounts of data recorded with that exact device, in a lab full of motion capture cameras and force plates.

00:00:40.402 --> 00:00:43.143
Build a new exoskeleton, and you start over.

00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:45.120
So we asked:

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.785
what if the controller never needed data from the device at all?

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:57.173
First, a virtual human with muscles learns to squat, imitating real people from an open dataset.

00:00:57.173 --> 00:01:00.814
As it moves, we record how hard its knee muscles work.

00:01:00.814 --> 00:01:07.490
Then a small neural network learns to predict that effort from only what the exoskeleton can sense:

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.052
its knee angle and two motion sensors.

00:01:11.366 --> 00:01:16.799
We built the exoskeleton from an off-the-shelf motor and 3D-printed carbon-fiber parts.

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:22.481
A hundred times a second, it estimates the wearer's knee effort and supplies a share of it.

00:01:23.766 --> 00:01:35.690
In seven people squatting, it cut the knee's peak effort by twenty-one and a half percent compared with no exoskeleton, and peak activity in a main thigh muscle by about twenty-seven percent.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.998
No training data came from the device or the wearers.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:47.085
The hardware, code, and training pipeline are open source, so any lab can build it and train their own controller.

00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:48.812
The details are in the paper.
